<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borsippa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Birs Nimrud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borsippa was an ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, about 20 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It was the cult centre of the god Nabu, whose temple, Ezida, was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>. The ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> at the site, Birs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, was once thought to be the Tower of Babel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> settled around the city in the 1st millennium BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a second and possibly a third stele were found with it, and over 50 manuscripts of the laws are known from Susa, Babylon, Nineveh, Assur, <b>Borsippa</b>, Nippur, Sippar, Ur, Larsa and other sites; together they fill in most of the stele's text, including much of the erased section.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucid king Antiochus I restored the temple of the god Nabu at <b>Borsippa</b> and commemorated the work in Akkadian cuneiform, in the manner of the Babylonian kings before him.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Bit-Dakkuri, 'House of Dakkuru', was one of the leading Chaldean tribes of Babylonia, settled along the Euphrates between Babylon and <b>Borsippa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-dakkuri">Bit-Dakkuri</a></li></ul></section>
